ath display="inline"><semantics><mrow>
 <mover accent="true">
  <mi>B</mi>
  <mo>→</mo>
 </mover>

</mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mover accent="true">
  <mi>B</mi>
  <mo>→</mo>
 </mover></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span>  and your right thumb points down the page, again as shown in the figure in the previous Worked Example. Thus, the direction of the force is in the  <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow>
 <mover accent="true">
  <mi>x</mi>
  <mo>^</mo>
 </mover>

</mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mover accent="true">
  <mi>x</mi>
  <mo>^</mo>
 </mover></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> -direction. The complete force is thus  <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow>
 <mrow>
  <mrow><mo>(</mo>
   <mrow>
    <mn>0.12</mn><mspace width="0.3em"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext></mrow>
  <mo>)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true">
   <mi>x</mi>
   <mo>^</mo>
  </mover>
  </mrow>
</mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mrow>
  <mrow><mo>(</mo>
   <mrow>
    <mn>0.12</mn><mspace width="0.3em"></mspace><mtext>N</mtext></mrow>
  <mo>)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true">
   <mi>x</mi>
   <mo>^</mo>
  </mover>
  </mrow></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> .</p>
</div></section>
      </details>
<div data-type="commentary" id="fs-id1167065086463"><div data-type="title">Discussion</div>
<p id="fs-id1167065086468">The direction of the force is the same as the initial direction of the force was in the previous example for a proton. However, because the current in a wire is confined to a wire, the direction in which the charges move does not change. Instead, the entire wire accelerates in the  <span class="os-math-in-para"><math display="inline"><semantics><mrow>
 <mover accent="true">
  <mi>x</mi>
  <mo>^</mo>
 </mover>

</mrow><annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content"><mover accent="true">
  <mi>x</mi>
  <mo>^</mo>
 </mover></annotation-xml></semantics></math></span> -direction. The force on a current-carrying wire in a magnetic field is the basis of all electrical motors, as we will see in the upcoming sections.</p>
</div>
</section></div></div></section></div></section>



<section data-depth="1" id="fs-id1167062376989" class="practice-problems"><h3 data-type="title">Practice Problems</h3><div data-type="exercise" id="fs-id1167062376996"><header></header><section>
<div data-type="problem" id="fs-id1167062377003">
<span class="os-number">1</span><span class="os-divider">. </span>
<div class="os-problem-container">
<!--<para id="fs-id1167065997185">
<link class="os-embed" url="#ost/api/ex/k12phys-ch20-ex009"/>
</para>-->
<p id="fs-id1164565223782">What is the magnitude of the force on an electron moving at 1.0 × 10<sup>6</sup> m/s perpendicular to a 1.0-T magnetic field?</p>
<ol id="fs-id1164566545989" type="a">
<li>0.8 × 10<sup>–13</sup> N</li>
<li>1.6 × 10<sup>–14</sup> N</li>
<li>0.8 × 10<sup>–14</sup> N</li>
<li>1.6 × 10<sup>–13</sup> N</li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</section></div>
<div data-type="exercise" id="fs-id1167065997196"><header></header><section>
<div data-type="problem" id="fs-id1167062346401">
<span class="os-number">2</span><span class="os-divider">. </span>
<div class="os-problem-container">
<!--<para id="fs-id1167062346403">
<link class="os-embed" url="#ost/api/ex/k12phys-ch20-ex010"/>
</para>-->
<p id="fs-id1164566534134">A straight 10 cm wire carries 0.40 A and is oriented perpendicular to a magnetic field. If the force on the wire is 0.022 N, what is the magnitude of the magnetic field?</p>
<ol id="fs-id1164566511235" type="a">
<li>1.10 × 10<sup>–2</sup> T</li>
<li>0.55 × 10<sup>–2</sup> T</li>
<li>1.10 T</li>
<li>0.55 T</li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</section></div>
</section>



<section data-depth="1" id="fs-id1167064853688" class="check-understanding"><h3 data-type="title">Check Your Understanding</h3>
<div data-type="injected-exercise" class="os-embed autogenerated-content" data-injected-from-nickname="" data-injected-from-version="8" data-injected-from-url="https://exercises.openstax.org/api/exercises?q=tag:k12phys-ch20-ex011" data-tags="os-practice-concepts k12phys-ch20 k12phys-ch20-s01 k12phys-ch20-s01-lo01 k12phys-ch20-ex011 lo:stax-phys:22-1-2 exid:k12phys-ch20-ex011 context-cnxmod:180e7ee2-fe4a-4d88-96aa-6cbfe8d1abb9 context-cnxmod:962ddd95-e1d0-41bc-8460-53416914502c context-cnxmod:ebbd4827-46f5-4e8f-82e9-5b8c44325738 aplo:stax-apphys:4.E.1.1 aplo:stax-apphys:2.D.4.1 dok:1 k12phys book:stax-phys book:stax-k12phys book:stax-apphys science-practice:modeling time:short blooms:4 filter-type:import:hs inbook-yes book-slug:college-physics-ap-courses module-slug:college-physics-ap-courses:22-1-magnets assignment-type:reading blooms:1" data-is-vocab="false">
    <div data-type="exercise-question" data-is-answer-order-important="true" data-formats="free-response multiple-choice" id="239493"><span class="os-number">3</span>
<span class="os-divider">. </span>

<div class="os-problem-container">
  
  
  <div data-type="question-stem">If two magnets repel each other, what can you conclude about their relative orientation?</div>
  <ol data-type="question-answers" type="a">
            <li data-type="question-answer" data-correctness="">
                <div data-type="answer-content">Either the south pole of magnet 1 is closer to the north pole of magnet 2 or the north pole of magnet 1 is closer to the south pole of magnet 2.</div>
                
            </li>
            <li data-type="question-answer" data-correctness="">
                <div data-type="answer-content">Either the south poles of both the magnet 1 and magnet 2 are closer to each other or the north poles of both the magnet 1 and magnet 2 are closer to each other.</div>
                
            </li>
            <li data-type="question-answer" data-correctness="">
                <div data-type="answer-content">Not enough information is given to draw any conclusion about the orientation of the magnets.</div>
                
            </li>
            </ol>
</div>

        
        
        </div>
    </div><div data-type="exercise" id="fs-id1167065914129" class="os-exercise"><header></header><section>
<div data-type="problem" id="fs-id1167065876216">
<span class="os-number">4</span><span class="os-divider">. </span>
<div class="os-problem-container">
<!--<para id="fs-id1167065876218">
<link class="os-embed" url="#ost/api/ex/k12phys-ch20-ex012"/>
</para>-->
<p id="fs-id1164566536651">Describe methods to demagnetize a ferromagnet.</p>
<ol id="fs-id1164565068226" type="a">
<li>by cooling, heating, or submerging in water</li>
<li>by heating, hammering, and spinning it in external magnetic field</li>
<li>by hammering, heating, and rubbing with cloth</li>
<li>by cooling, submerging in water, or rubbing with cloth</li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
</section></div>
<div data-type="injected-exercise" class="os-embed autogenerated-content" data-injected-from-nickname="" data-injected-from-version="7" data-injected-from-url="https://exercises.openstax.org/api/exercises?q=tag:k12phys-ch20-ex013" data-tags="k12phys-ch20 k12phys-ch20-s01 k12phys-ch20-s01-lo02 k12phys-ch20-ex013 lo:stax-phys:22-3-1 exid:k12phys-ch20-ex013 context-cnxmod:180e7ee2-fe4a-4d88-96aa-6cbfe8d1abb9 context-cnxmod:fd949c21-c297-455d-8046-6c9b4c7e9f46 context-cnxmod:3c462fff-87e8-49fa-8cef-3697efe55cb5 aplo:stax-apphys:2.D.3.1 dok:1 k12phys worked-example book:stax-phys book:stax-k12phys filter-type:import:has-context time:short assignment-type:homework filter-type:import:hs inbook-yes book-slug:college-physics-ap-courses module-slug:college-physics-ap-courses:22-3-magnetic-fields-and-magnetic-field-lines blooms:1" data-is-vocab="false">
    <div data-type="exercise-question" data-is-answer-order-important="true" data-formats="free-response multiple-choice" id="239495"><span class="os-number">5</span>
<span class="os-divider">. </span>

<div class="os-problem-container">
  
  
  <div data-type="question-stem">What is a magnetic field?</div>
  <ol data-type="question-answers" type="a">
            <li data-type="question-answer" data-correctness="">
                <div data-